{:edb/db-before-t 2025 :edb/db-after-t 2026 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535338 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:22:19.210Z" 13194139535338 true] [17592186166650 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062909/assets/0f4f0de23892a6f76642f8c7e5d3a6112a8ffa2d9f50000cd974f293a7d8b3b6.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." 13194139535338 true] [17592186166650 1100 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the circle shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3216/Source/Images/ed62e909ea502542ef307e57c0edb2a4.png)" 13194139535338 false] [17592186166650 1145 "Since our circle is centered at $(0,-1)$ and has a radius of $2$, its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-0)^2 + (y - (-1))^2 = 2^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x^2 + (y+1)^2 = 4\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + (y + 1)^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(4) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}((y + 1)^{2}) &= 0 \\\\\n2x + 2(y + 1)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 0 \\\\\n2(y + 1)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{x}{y + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535338 false] [17592186166650 1145 "Since our circle is centered at $(0,-1)$ and has a radius of $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-0)^2 + (y - (-1))^2 = 2^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx^2 + (y+1)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+1)^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2(y+1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535338 true] [17592186166651 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062909/assets/d3895bafe045f7e460887aee41bf390fdda753fed87eaab14f4a29ddb792267c.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." 13194139535338 true] [17592186166651 1100 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3216/Source/Images/fa26d004a0d7f5f86a31ea3f8071a534.png)" 13194139535338 false] [17592186166651 1145 "Since our ellipse is centered at the origin and has semiaxes $2$ and $4$, its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{4^2} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{16} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(1) \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{x^{2}}{4}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{y^{2}}{16}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{4} + \\frac{2y}{16} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y}{8} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{y}{8} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{x}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{4x}{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535338 false] [17592186166651 1145 "Since our ellipse is centered at the origin and has semiaxes $2$ and $4,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{4^2} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{16} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{4}+\\frac{2y}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535338 true] [17592186166652 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062909/assets/a92eae1ab0af29ea895b570c95199ece1e292c09953ebb2dabbb7c6e2d422d6c.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above." 13194139535338 true] [17592186166652 1100 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3216/Source/Images/6dfd6abbaf5d73a917c476962cd3cecf.png)" 13194139535338 false] [17592186166652 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O$, and passes through the point $(3,-2)$.\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny^2 = 4px\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 3$ and $y = -2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-2)^{2} &= 4p(3) \\\\\n4 &= 12p \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is\n\n$$\ny^2 = \\dfrac{4}{3}x\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y^{2}) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{4}{3}x) \\\\\n2y\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2}{3y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535338 false] [17592186166652 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(3,-2).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny^2 = 4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 3$ and $y = -2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 4 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\ny^2 = \\dfrac{4}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535338 true] [17592186166797 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344402 13194139535338 true] [17592186166797 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344409 13194139535338 true] [17592186166797 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344416 13194139535338 true] [17592186166797 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344423 13194139535338 true] [17592186166797 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344430 13194139535338 true] [17592186166798 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344437 13194139535338 true] [17592186166798 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344444 13194139535338 true] [17592186166798 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344451 13194139535338 true] [17592186166798 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344458 13194139535338 true] [17592186166798 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344472 13194139535338 true] [17592186166799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344465 13194139535338 true] [17592186166799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344479 13194139535338 true] [17592186166799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344482 13194139535338 true] [17592186166799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344486 13194139535338 true] [17592186166799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186344489 13194139535338 true] [17592186344402 1017 #edb/ref 17592186344403 13194139535338 true] [17592186344402 1089 "q-127493" 13194139535338 true] [17592186344402 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535338 true] [17592186344402 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062909/assets/f24d078ca0dc57ebd12778b7862683b0289e9c0d4c664639bd210dcc1f7c6d1b.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the circle shown above." 13194139535338 true] [17592186344402 1113 #uuid "e7bda6d9-2c04-5573-bcfc-a6e69eb3e22b" 13194139535338 true] [17592186344402 1145 "Since our circle is centered at the origin and has a radius of $4,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={4}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.\n$